The test itself sets `expected_exception_kind` from `ValueError.__name__` and uses `with pytest.raises(ValueError)`, so the expected kind is `"ValueError"`. The `xr.Dataset(...)` call passes two data variables on the same dimension `"x"`: `"alpha"` has length 3 and `"beta"` has length 2. `Dataset.__init__` delegates through `merge_data_and_coords`, which calls `calculate_dimensions`; that function records the first seen size for `"x"` from `"alpha"`, then raises `ValueError` when `"beta"` provides a different size. The error string is built directly by the f-string in `calculate_dimensions`, yielding the exact observed message below.

## Answer
{"expected_exception_kind":"ValueError","observed_exception_kind":"ValueError","observed_exception_message":"conflicting sizes for dimension 'x': length 2 on 'beta' and length 3 on {'x': 'alpha'}"}
